preConditionHook:
// THE SHALLOW PULLBACK THAT WAS REJECTED. The bar dips below the prior bar's
// low by no more than dipTicks AND closes back above that low (mirrored: pokes
// above the prior bar's high by no more than dipTicks and closes back below it).
// TP3-17's "the first pullback usually only falls below the low of the prior bar
// by a few ticks" (3) merged with T07-23's unbounded "aggressive bulls buy below
// the low of the prior bar" -- one rule, and a large dipTicks disables the
// bound.
//
// THE MERGED KEY WAS ShallowDipBelowPriorLow, RENAMED: one word apart from
// ShallowDipBelowPriorBarLow, which is a real and different rule in the same
// slice, and the name has to say what separates them.
//
// KEPT WHOLE, not split. Dipped-below and closed-back-above is one event -- a
// rejection -- in the way a crossing is one event: neither half is the claim.
// With the close term dropped this is exactly ShallowDipBelowPriorBarLow, and
// that is the rule to place when only the shallow dip is wanted; with the bound
// dropped it is DipBelowPriorBarLow. Neither of those, nor any pair of them,
// reaches this rule -- no rule in the corpus tests the close against the prior
// bar's low from above.
//
// Limit fills are mechanized at the close, filling at the next open, per the
// standing convention, so the entry gives up the intrabar fill; requiring the close back above is
// what makes that convention reasonable here. All three terms read bar 1
// unconditionally, so EL's lack of short-circuiting costs nothing.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local. Every scratch value is a localVariables entry,
// never a hook-scope declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
